# 78. Artemis Agent and Settings Management

Artemis should eventually be able to operate Agent and product settings in the
same canonical system used by a human operator.

## 78.1 Setting Metadata

Every Artemis-manageable setting should declare, where applicable:

- settingId
- canonical owner
- data type
- valid range or enum
- default
- current value
- scope
- consumer list
- secret-bearing status
- security sensitivity
- financial sensitivity
- runtime effect
- restart/reload requirement
- reversibility
- autonomy class
- user-lock state
- validation rules
- dependency constraints

## 78.2 Agent Configuration Examples

After the relevant Agent contract is verified, Artemis may be permitted to
perform bounded actions such as:

- Add an approved symbol
- Remove an approved symbol
- Change timeframe within an allowlist
- Adjust non-security analytical parameters within validated bounds
- Enable or disable an optional analytical input
- Change an approved model or provider preference

The exact Agent must remain the Source-of-Truth owner of its supported
configuration contract.

## 78.3 No Arbitrary Settings Mutation

Artemis must not use a generic patch interface that permits unknown or future
fields to be changed automatically.

New settings default to non-autonomous until classified.

## 78.4 Cross-Agent Changes

A change affecting multiple Agents has a larger blast radius than the same
change applied to one Agent.

Mass changes should use canary rollout, tighter limits and stronger approval
where appropriate.

## 78.5 Human Locks and Pinned Values

A human may mark a supported setting as:

- Artemis-managed
- Artemis-suggested only
- Approval-required
- Human-locked

Artemis must respect the effective lock.

A user may explicitly instruct Artemis to change a locked value only through the
canonical approval semantics for that action.

## 78.6 Temporary Optimization Changes

Where useful, Artemis may apply a time-bounded configuration experiment with an
automatic expiry or revert point.

The experiment must identify:

- Baseline
- Expected improvement
- Observation window
- Success criterion
- Failure criterion
- Revert rule

