# 79. Approval Integrity and Human Governance

Human approval is a security control and must not be reduced to a model-generated
question followed by an unbound `yes`.

## 79.1 Approval Artifact

A sensitive approval should bind to the exact intended action, including where
applicable:

- approvalId
- approving human identity
- Artemis actionId
- action type
- target resource
- normalized parameter hash or equivalent exact representation
- before/after diff
- risk/autonomy class
- relevant cost or amount
- environment
- expiry
- single-use or bounded-use semantics
- step-up-auth status
- policy version

Changing material action parameters invalidates the approval unless the approval
explicitly bounded those parameters.

## 79.2 Trusted Approval Presentation

Approval UI must be generated from the canonical Action Intent and trusted policy
metadata.

Do not allow untrusted web content, external advisor text or free-form model
output to define the authoritative action preview.

The user must be able to see what operation will actually occur.

## 79.3 User Instruction vs Approval

A natural-language request may express a goal without constituting approval for
every derived privileged action.

Example:

`Improve our market-data coverage`

does not automatically authorize:

- Paying a new provider
- Creating a credential
- Sending private data externally
- Deleting an existing provider
- Changing production security policy

A direct instruction may count as the required approval only when the policy
explicitly allows command-as-approval and the exact operation is sufficiently
specified and bound.

## 79.4 Strong Approval

Critical actions may require one or more of:

- Step-up authentication
- Re-authentication
- Two-person approval
- Separate security-owner approval
- Explicit owner approval immediately before execution

The same Artemis cognitive component that proposes an action must not fabricate
or self-issue the independent approval.

## 79.5 Approval Expiry and Replay Protection

Approvals must expire.

An old approval must not be replayed against a new state, new resource or new
parameter set.

